.TH LOCALPASSKEYD 8 "October 2026" "localpasskey 0.5.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
localpasskeyd \- software FIDO2/WebAuthn authenticator that stores passkeys on this computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B localpasskeyd
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-state\-dir
.IR directory ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B localpasskeyd
turns the computer into a passkey authenticator, similar to Windows Hello.
It is normally started by
.BR systemd (1)
as
.BR localpasskey.service .
.PP
The daemon creates a virtual USB security key through
.IR /dev/uhid .
Browsers and
.BR libfido2 (3)
programs talk to it over the FIDO CTAP2 protocol exactly as they would to a
hardware key. systemd's
.B fido_id
udev rules give the user of the active desktop session access to the
device node.
.PP
For each request the daemon finds the process that opened the device and
uses that user's passkeys. Before a passkey is created or used, a polkit
authentication dialog appears in that user's session and asks for the
user's own password (or anything else PAM accepts, such as a fingerprint).
Browsers that ask for user verification receive it, because the
authenticator advertises built-in user verification.
.PP
Browsers can also list and delete passkeys from their security-key settings
(CTAP credential management) once the user has set a PIN. The PIN alone is
not enough: the polkit dialog appears before a browser may manage passkeys,
and a passkey deleted this way goes to the trash, from which
.BR localpasskey (1)
can restore it. Eight wrong PINs in a row block the PIN, three in a row
block further attempts until the service restarts.
.PP
A user can have up to 4096 passkeys (including trashed ones), including several accounts for the
same site; for sign-ins without a user name the browser shows an account
chooser. Passkeys are device-bound: they are not synchronised, and they leave
the computer only in an export you ask for and approve; see
.BR localpasskey (1).
.PP
The daemon also keeps authenticator codes (TOTP, RFC 6238) for each user,
with their setup keys sealed like private keys, and computes the current
codes with its own clock. A setup key leaves the daemon only in an approved
export. Showing codes needs the user's password by default, through the
.B org.localpasskey.totp
action. A process that passes that dialog is unlocked: it may read codes
without a dialog until it has made no code request for 5 minutes, asks to
lock again, or exits. Unlocks are kept in memory only, so restarting the
daemon locks every process. Root is always unlocked. A user can turn the
password off for their own codes, which needs
.BR org.localpasskey.manage ;
then any of their processes can read their codes. A user can have up to 4096
authenticator codes (including trashed ones), apart from the passkey limit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/localpasskey/localpasskeyd.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " directory"
Store passkeys in
.IR directory .
Defaults to
.B $STATE_DIRECTORY
or
.IR /var/lib/localpasskey .
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Listen for
.BR localpasskey (1)
on
.IR path .
Defaults to
.IR control.sock
in
.B $RUNTIME_DIRECTORY
or
.IR /run/localpasskey .
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log debug messages, including the names of the sites involved.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/localpasskey/localpasskeyd.conf
Configuration: confirmation timeout, desktop notifications, log level and
the USB IDs of the virtual device.
.B notifications
turns all desktop notifications on or off;
.B activity_notifications
controls the ones shown when a passkey is saved, used, moved to the trash,
permanently deleted, exported or imported, or an authenticator code is
added, moved to the trash, permanently deleted, exported or imported, as opposed
to those about declined, timed-out or failed requests.
.TP
.I /var/lib/localpasskey/users/UID/credentials.json
The passkeys of the user with that uid, including those in the trash.
Readable by root only; private keys are additionally encrypted with
.IR /var/lib/localpasskey/master.key .
Back up both files together, or neither.
.TP
.I /var/lib/localpasskey/users/UID/pin.json
A hash of that user's PIN, encrypted with the same master key, and the
number of attempts left. Present only while a PIN is set.
.TP
.I /var/lib/localpasskey/users/UID/totp.json
That user's authenticator codes, including those in the trash, and whether
showing them needs the password. Readable by root only; setup keys are
encrypted with the same master key and bound to the uid. Present once the
user adds or imports a code, or changes that setting. Older versions of the
daemon ignore this file.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.localpasskey.policy
polkit actions
.B org.localpasskey.authenticate
(create or use a passkey),
.B org.localpasskey.manage
(move passkeys and authenticator codes to the trash, restore or permanently
delete them, add or change codes, import, set or remove the PIN, and stop
asking for the password before showing codes),
.B org.localpasskey.export
(export passkeys with their private keys and codes with their setup keys)
and
.B org.localpasskey.totp
(show authenticator codes).
.SH SECURITY
Approving the dialog is the only thing that stops a website from using
your passkeys, so do not grant
.B org.localpasskey.authenticate
without authentication in polkit rules.
.B org.localpasskey.totp
is the same for authenticator codes.
.PP
While a process has codes unlocked, or a user has turned the password off,
any process running as that user can read the current codes (not the setup
keys) without a dialog; an unlock also lets that process add, change, trash
and restore codes without asking, but never delete one permanently. Root and
anyone with
.I master.key
and the store can read every setup key. A backup of
.I /var/lib/localpasskey
holds the codes too, and is as sensitive as the passkeys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR localpasskey (1),
.BR localpasskey-bridge (1),
.BR polkit (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
